lane: okay-actor
why: most of the actor model is already assembled here and simply not
  called that -- a mailbox is a Channel (with backpressure and a menu
  of contracts, which typical actor libraries do not offer), sequential
  processing is one consumer, private state needs no locks because of
  it, spawning is Scheduler.fork, and okay-cluster already gives a
  REMOTE channel indistinguishable from a local one, which is location
  transparency.
  What is genuinely missing is SUPERVISION -- what happens when a
  handler throws -- plus `ask` (correlating a reply, with a timeout)
  and a children tree for stopping a subtree.
how: spec first, because restart policy is a DECISION and not an
  implementation detail: what happens to the message being handled
  when it throws, whether state is reset, what the sender sees.
  Then a thin okay-actor module in this repository's stated style
  ("nothing new is invented here"): behaviour as a function
  S => Msg => S ! Effects, state explicit, supervision a handler.
  It must NOT drag in actor orthodoxy -- untyped messages and hidden
  mutable state fight programs-as-values, typed effect rows and STM,
  and a module that fights the library is a foreign body.
